From: thc1006 <nctuie@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server: expose the speculative verification-step count per request

`server_slot_stats` already counts verification steps, increments the counter per slot, and uses
it to compute the `mean len` figure in the per-slot log line. The value is not in the JSON the
slot returns, so a client can read `draft_n` and `draft_n_accepted` but cannot reconstruct it.

The two are not interchangeable. `draft_n_accepted / draft_n` is the fraction of drafted tokens
that survived; the mean number of tokens emitted per verification step is
`1 + draft_n_accepted / draft_n_verif_steps`. The second is what decides whether speculation pays
off, because throughput scales with tokens produced per target-model pass, and the first does
not determine it: two runs with the same acceptance fraction and different draft widths produce
different mean lengths and different speedups.

`/metrics` already publishes the same count as `spec_decode_num_drafts_total`, but that is a
process-wide counter. It cannot be attributed to a request, so it is unusable for per-request
analysis or for any benchmark that changes conditions between requests.

There is no exact way to derive it from what the API returns today, and I found that the hard
way. `predicted_n - draft_n_accepted` looks right and is wrong by one, because the first
generated token comes from the prompt-processing pass rather than a decode forward.
`predicted_n - draft_n_accepted - 1` then reproduces the server's printed `mean len` on about
70 % of requests; the remainder need one fewer still, which is what truncation at `max_tokens`
looks like, and nothing in the response distinguishes those cases.

I ran a study on the wrong form without noticing. The numbers were plausible, stable across five
repetitions, and fit a linear cost model at r-squared 0.9998. What caught it was comparing the
derived figure against the `mean len` the server prints, which differed by a consistent -0.0204.
Correcting it moved the fitted coefficient by about 0.8 percent.

One line, no new state, no behaviour change. It reports what the server already knows.

---
 tools/server/server-common.cpp | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tools/server/server-common.cpp b/tools/server/server-common.cpp
--- a/tools/server/server-common.cpp
+++ b/tools/server/server-common.cpp
@@ -80,8 +80,9 @@ json server_slot_stats::to_json() const {
     };
 
     if (n_draft_tokens > 0) {
-        base["draft_n"]          = n_draft_tokens;
-        base["draft_n_accepted"] = n_draft_accepted;
+        base["draft_n"]             = n_draft_tokens;
+        base["draft_n_accepted"]    = n_draft_accepted;
+        base["draft_n_verif_steps"] = n_draft_verif_steps;
     }
 
     return base;
